## Findings

- [P1] Cache reuse is effectively disabled for normal UI runs. Cache entries are keyed by workflow, project, and node in [cache.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/cache.py:143), but the frontend omits `project_id` in [workflow.js](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/workflow/stores/workflow.js:280). The execution manager consequently generates a new project ID for every run in [execution.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/execution.py:89). Therefore an unchanged workflow rerun misses every cache entry unless it contains `project.existing`. The cache test masks this by hard-coding the same project ID for every run in [test_workflow_cache.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/tests/test_workflow_cache.py:63). This breaks both 4.2 acceptance criteria.

- [P1] `from_node`, `retry_failed`, and `retry_failed_desc` discard required upstream inputs. Their scopes contain only the target and descendants in [scheduler.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/scheduler.py:316). Execution then removes every edge crossing into that scope in [scheduler.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/scheduler.py:413), and resolves inputs solely from the remaining edges in [scheduler.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/scheduler.py:456). No prior outputs are restored. Consequently a failed TTS node retried alone receives no `script`, and a downstream join reached from one branch loses inputs from its other branch. Real adapters commonly index required inputs directly, such as [tts.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/adapters/tts.py:10). Existing tests check only calculated ID sets, not execution with boundary inputs.

- [P1] “Retry” is not tied to the failed execution being inspected. The panel passes only the mode and node ID in [ExecutionPanel.vue](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/workflow/components/ExecutionPanel.vue:115). `runWorkflow` submits the current workflow document, omits the failed execution’s `project_id`, and hard-codes `force: false` in [workflow.js](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/workflow/stores/workflow.js:280). Thus retrying a historical failure can use changed configuration and a new project rather than its recorded snapshot/artifacts. Additionally, every retry target goes through ordinary cache lookup in [scheduler.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/scheduler.py:499), so an older successful cache entry can suppress actual execution of the node being “retried.”

- [P2] Deep inspection cannot show actual resolved inputs or outputs. `_summarize` deliberately replaces strings with character counts, truncates mappings, and retains only three summarized list items in [scheduler.py](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/scheduler.py:185). Only these summaries are persisted, and the inspector’s expansion merely JSON-formats those summaries in [ExecutionPanel.vue](D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/workflow/components/ExecutionPanel.vue:90). A user therefore cannot inspect the concrete script, alignment, scene, or configuration values that caused a failure, contrary to step 4.4’s resolved-input/output inspection requirement.

Verification: all existing backend tests pass (`121 passed, 38 subtests`), the focused Phase 4 frontend tests pass (`41 passed`), and the production frontend build succeeds. No review changes were made.
